"""Serialize individually authored claims; this script makes no mathematical classifications."""
import json,pathlib
P=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
C=[]
F={57:'MPL_57_271-315_transcription.tex',58:'MPL_58_020-067_transcription.tex'}
def c(n,items,cote,scans,lines,words,statement,status,computation,cl,fate,refs,search,confidence='high'):
 C.append(dict(id=f'C-SA14-{n}',items=[f'SA14-{i}' for i in items],cote=cote,scans=scans,lines=[F[cote]+': '+lines],his_statement=words,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=computation,**{'class':cl},fate=fate,fate_date='Literature checked through 2026-11-19; circulation and publication distinguished in fate.',present_status='As of November 2026: '+fate,refs=refs,confidence=confidence,search_events=search))
def save():
 import re
 for record in C:
  record.setdefault('source_phrase',record['his_statement'])
  record['refs']=list(dict.fromkeys(record['refs']))
  excerpts=[]
  for locator in record['lines']:
   filename,ranges=locator.split(': ',1)
   src=(P.parents[2]/'sources'/'SA14_texts'/'pro'/filename)
   if not src.exists(): src=pathlib.Path('/workspace/scratch/1d52a94fe9c0/sources/SA14_texts/pro')/filename
   raw=src.read_text().splitlines()
   for a,b in re.findall(r'(\d+)[–-](\d+)',ranges):
    excerpts.append(filename+': '+a+'–'+b+'\n'+'\n'.join(raw[int(a)-1:int(b)]))
  record['his_statement']='\n\n'.join(excerpts)
 (P/'claims.json').write_text(json.dumps(C,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')

# Late cote57. Reused print is explicitly distinguished from handwriting.
c(201,[211],57,[271,273],'2–15,41–60','Diviseurs; diviseurs principaux','On a normal noetherian integral variety, codimension-one local rings are DVRs; rational functions give finite Weil divisors and linear equivalence is quotient by principal divisors. This is reused typescript, not a new Picard conjecture.','stated without proof','Definitions and the valuation-divisor construction; no numerical calculation.','E','Classical divisor theory, already used by the printed stratum. The supplied fragment does not identify its edition; Stacks0BE0 provides the modern Weil-divisor convention, and EGA II provides the geometric setting. No authorial priority is inferred.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII','StacksDivisors'],['PL-S01','PL-S17'])
c(202,[212],57,[272],'17–20','Je conjecture','For a good henselian local base, line bundles over the completion should have the same image as algebraic line bundles at every finite infinitesimal order. The page calls good a regular completion morphism, though its arrow is uncertain; it gives a henselization from complex algebraic geometry as an example.','open on the pages','Finite-presentation reduction to equations is explained by Artin; no assertion of exact equality of completed and algebraic Picard groups follows from approximation alone.','B','Artin1969 Theorems1.8/1.10 give approximation for henselizations essentially of finite type over a field or excellent DVR; the modern henselian G-ring statement is Stacks16.13.1. Applying it to a locally finitely presented Picard functor proves finite-order approximation in those settings. The written regular-completion criterion is retained; the exact hypotheses of the applied approximation theorem, rather than an undefined excellence label, are stated explicitly. Artin publication1969; no earlier circulation date established here.',['Artin1969Approximation','Stacks07QX'],['PL-S02'])
c(203,[212,251],57,[272],'22–25','support de R¹f_*O_X','The page proposes a stronger Picard comparison when R¹f_*O_X is supported at the closed point, but the target is transcribed ambiguously as completed Y or completed X.','open on the pages','No computation can decide the missing target; the formal/algebraic distinction is retained.','F','The exact arrow is not identifiable. Artin1969 supplies finite-order approximation; Elkik1973 III.1 supplies punctured-affine Picard bijectivity. Neither is silently substituted for this proper-morphism statement with an uncertain target. The source question remains an identification problem, not a certified open theorem.',['Artin1969Approximation','Elkik1973Henselian'],['PL-S02'],'medium')
c(204,[212],57,[272],'28–33','singularité isolée','For a henselian noetherian local ring A and its completion, compare Pic of the punctured spectra; the page asks under an isolated-singularity condition and proposes a resolution argument.','open on the pages','The supplied resolution argument is only a proposal; no computation is needed for the exact later theorem.','B','Elkik1973, III.1 Theorem3 pp.569–573, its Corollary1, following lemma and unnumbered corollary p.573 prove Pic(U)≅Pic(U′) for every noetherian henselian pair(A,J), U=SpecA minus V(J), U′ its inverse image in SpecÂ. This includes the question and removes isolated singularity. The paper credits Boutot for earlier restricted cases, but those earlier proofs were not opened. Publication1973; receipt30June1973 is not a circulation date.',['Elkik1973Henselian'],['PL-S02'])
c(205,[214,217],57,[275,279,280],'68–78,109–141','Pic/Pic^τ','The numerical quotient is Pic/Pic^τ, rather than Pic/Pic⁰; over an algebraically closed field for proper X, Pic^τ is the union of components mapping to torsion in NS.','stated without proof','For any abelian group NS, (NS/NS_tors) is torsion-free; this checks the quotient convention, not relative representability.','A','FGA VI complement(viii), pp.305–306, equates τ-equivalence with numerical degree zero on all integral curves; complement(vi) gives finite generation of geometric NS. Published1962. The quotient convention is thereby identified exactly; global relative representability is a separate claim.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement'],['PL-S03'])
c(206,[215],57,[275,276],'68–90','torseur; fidèlement plat','A representable quotient Pic/Pic^τ is locally described by Pic^τ-cosets and torsor descent. The literal placement of every coset inside Pic^τ is impossible for a nonidentity class.','false as stated','Identity-coset test: a nontrivial quotient element has a coset disjoint from the kernel. Its ambient is Pic. This is a logical counterexample independent of scans.','F','The general torsor mechanism is standard, but the literal ambient-space sentence must be checked on ink. Kleiman2005 Lemma4.9 proves a precise representability descent theorem under smooth proper equivalence-relation hypotheses; it does not license replacing an arbitrary quotient sheaf by a scheme.',['Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(207,[215],57,[277],'92–103','séparé; non ramifié','For a representable group quotient with faithfully flat quotient map, closedness of the kernel controls separatedness; the page adds an unramified assertion and a proper/quasi-projective representability criterion.','proof sketched','Closedness follows by pulling back the diagonal to the kernel relation. The unramified and scheme-existence clauses require their printed hypotheses, not just pointwise cosets.','C','The closed-kernel diagonal criterion is established by descent. Kleiman2005 Lemma4.9 gives scheme representability for the specific quasi-projective smooth proper relation; FGA236 §1 treats group components. The source has no complete theorem covering all its quotient clauses, so only these precisely bounded parts are identified. No claim of an open general quotient theorem is made.',['Kleiman2005Picard','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(208,[216],57,[277],'100–101','il suffirait','The margin hopes that flatness and closedness of Pic^τ suffice to represent the Néron quotient, possibly without representing all of Pic.','open on the pages','No new quotient construction is attempted.','C','Modern algebraic-space Picard representability (Kleiman2005 Remark4.18, Artin theorem as stated there) and flat group quotients explain an algebraic-space route, but an algebraic-space quotient is not a scheme. The exact scheme-valued formulation without full Pic representability was not located in the opened sources. This is a scope/identification remainder, not evidence of an unsolved theorem.',['Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(209,[218],57,[281],'143–153','nξ=n′ξ′','Ample classes are grouped by equality of positive integral multiples; the relation is positive rational proportionality, and high positive powers of an ample line bundle are very ample on a proper scheme over a field.','stated without proof','Transitivity uses multiplication of the positive multipliers; positivity excludes opposite rays.','E','The definition and elementary equivalence relation are standard; EGA II §§4.5–4.6, especially Proposition4.6.11, gives eventual very ampleness under the quasi-compact finite-type hypotheses; the proper-over-a-field case satisfies them. No novel integral primitive-class assertion is present. EGA II published1961, manuscript date not determined.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII'],['PL-S17'])
c(210,[203],57,[283],'159–190','0→Pic_Y→Pic_X','For g:X→Y universally Stein with a section, the relative Picard sequence has kernel Pic(Y), and restriction to the section splits the Pic(Y) factor; the residual term is the pointed relative Picard functor.','proof sketched','Rigidification at the section removes pullbacks from Y. Our proof-check P22 records the sheaf-level splitting and why it is not only a pointwise identity.','A','FGA Picard descent is the published framework; the precise product decomposition is obtained by rigidification and the representing property, as independently checked in P22. FGA236 alone was not found to state this exact formula verbatim. ClassA identifies the published Picard-functor machinery, not an unverified theorem number.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(211,[203],57,[283,284],'159–231','Hom_pté(Y,Pic_Z/S)','For pointed proper factors Y,Z over S with universal O-pushforward equal to O_S and a represented Picard functor, rigidified bundles on Y×Z split into two Picard factors and pointed maps Y→Pic_Z/S.','proof sketched','P22 checks the restriction/rigidification inverse maps; geometric points alone would not prove the relative sheaf identity.','A','A formal consequence of Grothendieck’s relative Picard representing property and rigidification; published Picard functor definitions are in FGA and Kleiman2005 §2. The exact source formula is checked independently, while no unsupported FGA theorem locator is invented.',['Kleiman2005Picard','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(212,[219,252,249],57,[284,286],'192–231,251–289','(A′×B)/Γ','The quotient-product formula uses Γ-equivariant pointed morphisms into the dual abelian scheme. If it is read for full Pic(A), replacing Pic(A) by A′ loses NS(A); an identity/torsion-component restriction is needed.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P22: Γ=1 and B a point reduces the full-Pic formula to Pic(A)=A′, contradicted by any ample class on a positive-dimensional abelian variety.','C','The equivariant identity-component mechanism is standard descent, and Oda1969 confirms the importance of full group-scheme structure. The transient full-Pic replacement at line208 fails; the NS sequence and Pic^τ formula at lines212–219 restore the distinction, and the full Pic factor is retained again at lines258–279. The component qualifier and the stronger relative Hom-kernel reduction remain conditional readings, not an established family theorem.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S03','PL-S04'])
c(213,[221,247,256],57,[285],'233–249','Y′×A′[2]','For an abelian scheme A acted on by inversion and a free translation action of constant Z/2 on B, the indicated quotient gives Pic^τ=Y′×A′[2] in the stated component setting. In characteristic2 its tangent dimension is dimY+dimA, whereas its dimension is dimY.','proof sketched','scripts/calculations.py: igusa_tangent_control; outputs/calculations.json; proof_checks P11. Multiplication2 on an abelian scheme is finite locally free, so the example is flat but nonsmooth in characteristic2.','A','FGA236 §2.9 discusses Igusa-type nonreduced Picard schemes and a distinct Mumford nonflat family. The page’s absolute inversion/translation calculation is consistent with that theory; it does not itself prove nonflatness. The literal Γ≅B arrow is impossible for positive-dimensional B and is kept in Stage4.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S04'])
c(214,[222,247],57,[287],'291–316','h^{0,1}; h^{1,0}','The equivariant product calculation expresses H¹(O) and H⁰(Ω¹) through invariant spaces; the page seeks to exploit unequal invariant dimensions. The basic inversion control does not itself produce a Hodge-number difference in characteristic2.','proof sketched','scripts/calculations.py: igusa_tangent_control; P11 separates the two tangent representations and computes the inversion control.','C','Oda1969 Corollaries5.11–5.12 give the exact absolute H¹_dR/Dieudonné comparison over a perfect field, with a stable subspace in the general smooth proper case. They do not supply the unspecified relative examples. The invariant-space strategy is valid in its descent setting, while the proposed family realization is not identified.',['Oda1969Dieudonne','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S04'])
c(215,[222],57,[288],'318–320','4 exemples','Four pathologies of Hodge/Picard behavior are announced as desired examples; no construction is carried out on this face.','open on the pages','No numerical instance is supplied; P11 is only a control against overclaiming.','F','FGA236 §2.9 verifies nonreduced and nonflat Picard phenomena, and Oda1969 gives absolute comparison statements. The four terse desired examples are not sufficiently specified to certify four exact literature matches. This is bounded non-identification, not a novelty or open-problem claim.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S04'],'medium')
c(216,[223,253,246],57,[289,290],'322–368','pF=G; F₁⊂G₁','For finite flat subgroups F,G of X[p], the induced quotient homomorphism may become p-divisible precisely when the required subgroup containment holds. The displayed equalities/inclusions are inconsistent if taken literally.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py: p_subgroup_equality, tate_isogeny_test; P10. For nonzero order-p G and F⊂X[p], pF=0≠G; generic F⊂G is also incompatible with the proposed generic obstruction.','C','The conditional subgroup criterion is independently verified. Maulik–Poonen2012 Proposition3.6 and Example3.12 establish genuine p-primary specialization defects, but by a different CM/isogeny construction. They do not repair the manuscript’s arrows or instantiate its unspecified family. First arXiv27July2009; publication2012.',['MaulikPoonen2012NS'],['PL-S05'])
c(217,[223],57,[289,290],'322–368','divisible par p; N.S.','In smooth proper families, geometric NS specialization can have p-primary failure of saturation in residue characteristic p; the proposed construction uses a homomorphism becoming p-divisible.','proof sketched','P10 checks the conditional quotient argument; tate_isogeny_test illustrates saturation, not existence of the written family. The Hom factor for A×B′ is Hom(A,B), not Hom(A,B′).','B','Maulik–Poonen2012 Proposition3.6 gives injectivity and torsion-free cokernel after inverting p, and Example3.12 gives nonzero p-torsion in the cokernel. This exactly establishes the phenomenon, while the manuscript family remains conditional. ArXiv first27July2009; publication2012.',['MaulikPoonen2012NS'],['PL-S05'])
c(218,[228],57,[291],'370–402','M[p] fini','A p-primary torsion abelian group with finite p-torsion is a finite p-group plus a finite direct sum of Prüfer groups. The statement concerns abstract groups, not finite flat group schemes.','stated without proof','P9 checks the finite-cotype structure through Pontryagin duality/finitely generated Z_p-modules; finite tests in tate_lattice_tests are illustrative.','E','The structure is the elementary torsion dual of finitely generated Z_p-modules. Oda1969 uses a much richer Dieudonné category for group schemes; it is not an attribution of this elementary result nor a license to replace a group scheme by its points. No original historical source is uniquely identified from the page.',['Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S06'])
c(219,[228,231],57,[291,292],'370–423','T_p(M); T^p(M)','The page defines T_p(M)=Hom(M,Q_p/Z_p), contravariantly, and T^p(M)=lim M[pⁿ], covariantly. For divisible finite-corank M these are finite free Z_p-lattices of the same rank, naturally dual.','stated without proof','P9 checks variance on multiplication-p maps and the finite part; tate_lattice_tests stores the finite-level control.','E','These elementary Tate/character-module constructions are established. Oda1969 Corollary5.11 instead compares the Dieudonné module of the full p-torsion group scheme of an abelian variety with H¹_dR; that exact comparison must not be confused with either abstract lattice here.',['Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S06'])
c(220,[229],57,[292],'403–413','groupes finis','The isogeny category obtained from finite-cotype p-primary groups by killing finite groups corresponds to finite-dimensional Q_p-vector spaces, after rationalizing the associated Z_p-lattice.','stated without proof','P9 and tate_isogeny_test: multiplication p has finite kernel on Q_p/Z_p and becomes invertible only after tensoring with Q_p.','E','Standard isogeny-category linear algebra. The literal lattice category without rationalization would be false; the needed Q_p tensor is retained as an explicit mathematical clarification, not an ink edit. Oda’s rationalization context supports the category distinction but is not claimed as the first proof.',['Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S06'])
c(221,[230],57,[292],'414–423','H fini; quotient direct','For a morphism between finite-corank divisible p-primary groups, a split epimorphism is equivalent to the corresponding lattice direct-summand criterion and to surjectivity on Hom(H,−) for every finite p-group H, or for one nonzero such H.','stated without proof','P8 proves the Smith-normal-form criterion. scripts/calculations.py:tate_lattice_tests; H=0 is explicitly excluded because it detects nothing.','E','This is the elementary Z_p-matrix criterion for split surjectivity, not a general assertion for every finite-cotype group. The source’s divisible-category restriction is preserved. Searches through Tate/Dieudonné references found no reason to treat it as a new theorem.',['Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S06'])
c(222,[224,254],57,[294,297],'425–448,499–539','H²(X₀,O_X₀); c₁','For a square-zero deformation, a line bundle has an obstruction in H² of the square-zero ideal; the stated cup-product expression pairs its first Chern class with the deformation class. Possible lifts form an H¹-torsor.','proof sketched','P23 derives the Čech obstruction from lifted transition functions and explains the cup-product identification. The ambiguous source degree is not changed.','A','FGA236 §2.6 gives the square-zero lifting obstruction and the H¹/H² groups, including the prime-to-characteristic lifting argument. Oda1969 Corollary5.12 is an absolute comparison with a specified stable subspace, not a relative lifting theorem. PublicationFGA1962; Oda1969, received22April1968.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S04','PL-S06'])
c(223,[224,233,235],57,[294,297,311],'425–448,499–539,919–933','Pic^{00|∞}','The page asks for a relative p-divisible/reduced Picard part over Artinian or reduced bases and for lifting along a deformation. Its superscript and some arrows remain uncertain.','open on the pages','P23 separates lifting points, lifting subgroup schemes and flatness; none implies the others merely by divisibility on geometric points.','C','Oda1969 Proposition4.9 isolates the p-divisible Albanese contribution and finite p-torsion defects; Corollaries5.11–5.12 give absolute comparison over a perfect field. These settle the absolute distinction but do not answer the unspecified relative existence/flatness question. FGA236 §2.9 supplies an obstruction to blanket Picard flatness.',['Oda1969Dieudonne','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S04','PL-S06'],'medium')
c(224,[241],57,[295],'450–465','τ″=(τ+τ′)/(1+δττ′)','In k[S]/(S²−δ), multiplication yields the displayed rational parameter law and multiplicative norm ξ²−δη², wherever the denominators are units. Reused printed algebra.','proved there','scripts/calculations.py:diangles; exact symbolic residuals zero. P19 states the domain and denominator conditions.','E','An elementary identity in a quadratic algebra, independently checked; no historical priority is inferred. Bibliographic searches did not identify the exact reused edition. The valid formulas themselves are fully determined by the supplied text.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII'],['PL-S17'])
c(225,[244],57,[296],'467–478','E₂^{pq}=lim^(p) Ext^q','For a filtered direct system(A_α), there is a spectral sequence R^p lim Ext^q(A_α,B)⇒Ext^{p+q}(colim A_α,B), with the categorical hypotheses required for the standard double-complex construction. Reused printed fragment.','stated without proof','P24 explains the injective-resolution/Roos-complex construction and the role of exact filtered colimits; the missing preceding proposition is not invented.','B','Laudal1965 develops the derived-limit framework and cites Roos1961 and the two1962 notes. The exact preceding hypotheses of this detached sheet remain absent, so the modern filtered module formulation is stated as reconstruction. No date for the physical reused sheet is inferred.',['Laudal1965Limits'],['PL-S07'],'medium')
c(226,[244],57,[296],'477–487','p>dimΛ−k','The printed induction claims high derived-limit vanishing over a regular ring for a filtered system of finitely generated modules, and explicitly denies extension to arbitrary indexing categories or arbitrary modules.','proof sketched','The displayed page only supplies the final induction step; its L_k and theorem1 are undefined here. No computational substitution can recover those definitions.','F','The Roos1962 biduality notes are identified in Laudal1965’s bibliography, but their exact theorem and the missing L_k definitions were not obtained. The statement is not classified as an unbounded vanishing theorem. This is a precise source-identification limit, after backward bibliography searches.',['Laudal1965Limits'],['PL-S07'],'medium')
c(227,[244],57,[296],'488–497','deux suites spectrales','A double complex gives spectral sequences R^p lim Ext^q(A,M_α) and Ext^p(A,R^q lim M_α) toward a common total cohomology, subject to the module-resolution hypotheses; the page questions nonfiltered indexing.','proof sketched','P24 gives the double-complex construction for finite-type A over a noetherian ring and the boundedness needed to interchange products with finite free resolutions.','B','The derived-limit literature opened through Laudal1965 supplies this framework. The finite-generation and regular finite-dimensional specialization on the sheet is preserved; an arbitrary-object derived functor assertion is not claimed. Roos’s exact original page remains a bibliographic lead.',['Laudal1965Limits'],['PL-S07'],'medium')
c(228,[236],57,[298,300],'541–592,608–623','pas quasi-affine','Gluing two punctured affine planes along punctured coordinate axes via t↦t and t↦t⁻¹ forces every global function to be constant on the common G_m, so the resulting separated pinched scheme is not quasi-affine.','proof sketched','scripts/calculations.py:glued_curve_restrictions; P12 proves k[t]∩k[t⁻¹]=k and the failure to separate two points.','B','Ferrand2003 Theorem5.4 and §6 provide the modern pushout framework for finite pinching under the affine-neighborhood hypothesis. The global-function obstruction is independently checked. The exact elementary two-plane example was not found verbatim, so no false attribution to Ferrand is made.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08'])
c(229,[237],57,[298,300],'574–592,608–623','vérifier','The swap/inversion involution is proposed to yield a quotient with the stated quasi-affine behavior; existence of suitable common affine orbit neighborhoods is left to be checked.','open on the pages','No quotient computation is claimed beyond the verified Laurent restriction argument P12.','C','Ferrand2003 Theorem7.1 supplies a precise affine-neighborhood criterion for pinching/quotient constructions. It does not by itself prove the page’s missing orbit-neighborhood check or identify the quotient as quasi-affine. The remaining work is an explicit example check, not a known open general theorem.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08'],'medium')
c(230,[245],57,[299],'594–602','lemme de Zariski','The editorial Zariski lemma concerns constants in a rational-function extension: if K⊂E, X is a tuple algebraically independent over E and u=a(X)/b(X)∈E(X) is algebraic over K(X), then normalized coprime a,b have coefficients algebraic over K. Equivalently the relative algebraic closure of K(X) in E(X) is K₀(X), where K₀ is the relative algebraic closure of K in E.','stated without proof','P25 completes the written finite-orbit proof, normalizing coprime numerator and denominator before passing from the finite orbit of u to algebraicity of its coefficients.','E','This is a classical relative-constants lemma, explicitly credited to Chevalley as a proof of a Zariski lemma. Searches by the familiar name mainly returned the different finite-type-field lemma, which is not a match. P25 independently verifies the actual coefficient statement. Stacks037P supplies neighboring relative-constant/geometric-irreducibility theory, not a false exact citation to the finite-type-field theorem. The exact Bourbaki edition is unlocated.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(231,[245],57,[299],'600–606','algébriquement fermé; car.0','If k is relatively algebraically closed in a field extension K in characteristic0, then K/k is regular; this geometric integrality property is preserved after base extension, with tensor product interpreted correctly.','stated without proof','P25 explains that K⊗k k′ need not itself be a field; take its domain/fraction-field form and retain the regular-extension condition.','E','Standard regular-field-extension theory. The printed editorial sentence is not interpreted as saying every tensor product is a field. The Stacks geometric-integrality criteria provide a verified modern formulation; no exact old proof attribution is supplied.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(232,[245],57,[301],'625–641','dérivations; p-bases','For an exponent-one field extension K/k in characteristic p with p-basis(b_i), Ω_{K/k} has basis db_i and derivations are determined freely by their values on the p-basis.','stated without proof','P25 derives the monomial basis and differentiates the relations b_i^p∈k; rank equals p-basis cardinality.','E','Classical p-basis/differential theory, appearing as an exposition task rather than a new Picard result. Modern Stacks algebra supplies the matching p-basis criterion. The source does not furnish an arbitrary-exponent generalization.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(233,[245],57,[301],'635–637','Kummer; Artin–Schreier–Witt','The sheet asks for treatment of Kummer and Artin–Schreier–Witt theory, without a precise new proposition.','open on the pages','No explicit calculation or specific theorem is asserted on the surviving lines.','F','These are named classical theories, but the intended exposition task is not a uniquely identifiable claim. Searches of Stacks and the source bibliography verify the subject only; no modern theorem is substituted for absent quantifiers.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'],'high')
c(234,[238],57,[302],'649–654','Pic X=0','Pinch two projective spaces along a connected proper Y with independent hyperplane restrictions. If the pushout exists and Γ(Y,O_Y)=k, the units sequence gives Pic=ker(Z²→PicY)=0, hence the proper positive-dimensional result is nonprojective although its normalization is projective.','proof sketched','scripts/calculations.py:elliptic_gluing_pic; P13. An elliptic curve with two degree3 bundles of nontorsion difference gives the required independence; existence is over a field admitting such a point.','B','Ferrand2003 §6.3 p.575 treats exactly two projective spaces pinched along a common closed subscheme and proves projectivity iff positive powers of the two hyperplane restrictions agree. The zero-Pic specialization additionally uses the independently checked units argument. Publication2003; received16December2002, not a first-circulation date.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08'])
c(235,[245],57,[303],'656–677','exposant1; p-base','A finite purely inseparable exponent-one extension has degree p^r for a finite p-basis of length r, and its derivations admit the corresponding coordinate description. The October1957 date belongs to Tribu n°43/rédaction267.','stated without proof','P25 verifies the p-monomial basis of size p^r and the derivative basis.','E','Classical field theory in a dated editorial/Bourbaki stratum. The date is documentary metadata, not a date for the neighboring Picard manuscript. Stacks differentials supplies the modern p-basis formulation.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(236,[239],57,[304],'679–722','diviseurs; recollement','In a finite pinching X′=X amalgamated with S along Y, invertible modules are pairs of invertible modules on X and S with an isomorphism on Y; Cartier-divisor formulations require the usual nonzerodivisor and compatibility conditions.','proof sketched','P14 proves affine fibre-product module patching and the line-bundle descent sequence; no closed-cover Čech exactness is assumed without the fibre-product ring condition.','B','Ferrand2003 §2.2 gives flat-module patching and Theorem5.4 gives pushout existence under the finite affine-neighborhood hypotheses. This validates the precise pinching model and distinguishes it from arbitrary descent on a closed cover.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08'])
c(237,[239,248],57,[304],'679–722','O_Y^*/O_S^*','For the same pinching, units and Picard groups fit Γ(X)^*×Γ(S)^*→Γ(Y)^*→Pic(X′)→Pic(X)×Pic(S)→Pic(Y); the quotient of units supplies the gluing kernel.','proof sketched','P14 derives the exact sequence. scripts/calculations.py:nodal_graph_genus checks the two-section P¹ control, whose identity-component kernel is G_m.','B','Ferrand2003 flat-module patching supplies the exact descent basis; the nodal control is an independently worked specialization, not claimed to appear on scan304. This is established pinching theory.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08'])
c(238,[240],57,[306],'733–752','X×P→P','From a family of translates x·D linearly equivalent to D, the author proposes a finite-dimensional stable linear system and a morphism X×P→P. The operation and action hypotheses are not specified on this face.','open on the pages','Finite-dimensionality of H⁰(L) for a proper scheme is insufficient alone to construct a group action on P(H⁰L); compatible linearizations/projective action are additional data.','F','FGA/Kleiman represents linear systems under explicit proper-flat and cohomology/base-change hypotheses. The unspecified operation here prevents identification of the stronger stability assertion. This is a missing-data problem, not a certified open boundedness theorem.',['Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(239,[242],57,[307],'754–762','Proposition7.2','If G,H are finitely presented group schemes over S, G is S-flat, all residue characteristics are zero, and u:G→H is a group monomorphism, then u is an immersion. Reused printed proposition.','proof sketched','The supplied fibre/reducedness argument is compared with the complete published proof; no computational experiment needed.','A','Exact match: SGA3, ExposéVIII, Proposition7.2, following the DVR reduction and Cartier reducedness argument. The opened typeset primary reedition contains the full statement and proof. Original SGA3 seminar1962–1964, publication1970; no date for this reused physical sheet is inferred.',['DemazureGrothendieck1970SGA3II'],['PL-S18'])
c(240,[255],57,[309,310],'771–782,881–885','réunion disjointe; non','The page asks whether the relative Picard scheme decomposes into finite-type pieces/connected components; a later margin says connected components need not be finite type over S.','corrected by himself later (both places)','The two source loci distinguish geometric-fibre components from global connected components. No example is supplied on these faces.','A','FGA236 §2 and its complement distinguish finite-type Pic^τ from the entire Picard scheme. Kleiman2005 Theorem6.20 gives a finite-type open-and-closed decomposition by Hilbert polynomial for projective flat geometrically integral families, which is stronger-hypothesis positive evidence, not a general connected-component theorem. The manuscript’s negative general answer is retained without inventing its counterexample.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(241,[255],57,[309],'775–782','adhérence; quasi-compact','The question concerns quasi-compactness of closures of the relevant Picard pieces; its precise component and base hypotheses are not fully specified.','open on the pages','No exact morphism or test example is defined.','F','Kleiman2005 Theorems6.16 and6.20 give finite-type, hence quasi-compact, pieces under projective flat geometrically integral hypotheses. They do not identify the unspecified closure assertion. Searches in FGA and the modern exposition leave a reading/quantifier problem.',['Kleiman2005Picard','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(242,[214,226,255],57,[309,310,314],'778–782,839–840,991–993','fermé si f normal','For proper flat geometrically normal families with represented Picard functor, Pic⁰ is proper and closed and Pic^τ is closed in Pic.','stated without proof','Normality is a morphism hypothesis (geometric normal fibres), not merely normality of the total space.','A','FGA236 §2.3 states the normal-proper Picard closedness/properness results in the represented setting. Its §3.3/3.5 records the related hypotheses. Published1962. Dropping representability or replacing fibrewise normality by total-space normality is not covered by this match.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'])
c(243,[255],57,[309,310,311],'789–791,846–855,919–920','universellement ouvert','The questions ask universal openness of Pic^τ→S, with proposed affirmative loci of prime-to-residue-characteristic torsion and characteristic0 in the normal setting.','open on the pages','Prime-to-characteristic multiplication is étale, but translating this into base openness requires the stated group-component hypotheses.','C','FGA236 §2.5 proves prime-to-characteristic multiplication étale; §§2.7–2.10 and3.5 give appropriate smoothness/variation results. These settle the stated tame/smooth settings, not every uncertain universal-openness clause. The marginal all-characteristic question is not resolved by the Igusa absolute example.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(244,[235,255],57,[309,310],'789–791,842–844','plat? Non [Mumford]','The unconditional flatness hope for relative Pic^τ is rejected by an annotated Mumford counterexample.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P11 shows why Y′×A′[2] is flat and therefore is not itself this nonflat counterexample.','A','FGA236 §2.9 records Mumford’s nonflat Picard family over an Artinian base built from Igusa’s example. This is an exact published fate of the negative answer, distinct from absolute nonreducedness. Published1962.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S04'])
c(245,[255],57,[309,310],'793–796,857–859','Pic^τ de type fini: Oui','For a proper family with the Picard functor represented and the standard finite-presentation hypotheses, Pic^τ is of finite type over the base.','stated without proof','Finite type of Pic^τ does not imply the whole Picard scheme or all its global connected components are finite type.','A','SGA VI XIII Theorem4.7(iii), pp.647–648, proves finite type over a noetherian base for every proper X/S. FGA236 complement(v), pp.304–305, states the general finite-type result; Kleiman2005 Remark6.19 explains the reduction and Theorem6.16 proves the projective geometrically integral case. PublicationFGA1962; modern exposition arXiv1April2005.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(246,[255,226],57,[309,313],'798–799,950–968','séparé si représentable?','The pages ask whether represented Pic or Pic^τ is separated, then distinguish geometrically locally integral/separable fibre hypotheses and cite a double-elliptic-fibre nonseparated Pic⁰ example.','open on the pages','A representable group functor can be nonseparated over a base; representability alone is not a separation proof. The specific double-fibre example is not reconstructed from its label.','C','Kleiman2005 §4.18 and Example4.14 discuss representability limits and nonseparated curve-family Picard behavior. FGA236 normal-family hypotheses yield separatedness/properness of the relevant component. The exact double-elliptic example and every weakened fibre condition are not identified in the opened primary pages.',['Kleiman2005Picard','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(247,[226,255],57,[309,313],'800–805,970–976','dim Pic; α+μ+λ','The dimension of geometric Picard fibres is queried, with a distinction between general upper semicontinuity and constancy in the absence of additive components.','open on the pages','The sum α+μ+λ is the dimension of the smooth reduced connected algebraic group; scheme tangent dimension is a different invariant.','A','FGA236 §2.7 proves upper semicontinuity and the specified locally constant case without additive components. §2.10 separates h¹(O) from Picard dimension and characterizes equality by smoothness over a field. Published1962.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03','PL-S04'])
c(248,[255],57,[309],'803–805','rang ℓ-adique','The page suggests local constancy of an ℓ-adic Picard rank in the normal/proper setting, with ℓ prime to residue characteristics.','open on the pages','The Tate rank of the reduced connected Picard group is 2α+μ; it is not NS rank, which may jump.','C','FGA236 §§2.5–2.8 gives the tame torsion and variation framework. Exact local constancy needs the page’s normal/proper and representability hypotheses; it is not a statement that all NS ranks are constant. The ambiguous full formulation is retained as a bounded partial match.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','MaulikPoonen2012NS'],['PL-S03','PL-S05'],'medium')
c(249,[203,255],57,[309],'807–808','Pic^τ(X×Y)','For suitable pointed proper geometrically connected factors, the torsion component of the Picard functor of the product is the product of the two torsion components; the correspondence factor has torsion-free discrete geometric part.','stated without proof','P22 proves the product splitting and explains why a torsion homomorphism between abelian varieties is zero; relative sheaf hypotheses remain explicit.','C','The smooth proper geometric case follows from the published Picard formalism and rigidity of homomorphisms between abelian varieties, checked in P22. For the broader relative functor the representing, base-change and rigidity hypotheses remain necessary to match the written formula; no exact FGA236 numbered theorem was located.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(250,[209,255],57,[309],'810–817','Isom? surjectif?','The page asks for hyperplane restriction on a Picard object, but the superscript and direction are uncertain.','open on the pages','No dimension-free isomorphism is asserted: surfaces and curves already require separate treatment.','A','SGA2 XI§§1–3 and XII give precise Picard restriction results under Lef/Leff, conormal H¹/H² vanishing and depth/dimension hypotheses; XI3.18 is a concrete global formulation. Seminar1962, publication1968. The manuscript arrow remains a Stage4 question rather than being replaced by whichever published version is true.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'],'medium')
c(251,[255],57,[310],'849–855','n premier aux caractéristiques: étale','On a represented relative Picard functor of a proper flat family, multiplication by n is étale wherever n is invertible on the base.','stated without proof','FGA236 §2.6 square-zero obstruction groups are killed by invertible n; this is the deformation check, not a pointwise separability argument.','A','Exact match FGA236 Proposition2.5 and its proof2.6. Published1962. The theorem is only prime to all relevant residue characteristics.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'])
c(252,[255],57,[310],'849–855,861–871','n quelconque; ouverte (?)','The author asks whether multiplication by arbitrary n is universally open on Pic^τ; a nearby negative margin has uncertain attachment.','open on the pages','For G_a in characteristic p, [p] is constant and is not open on a positive-dimensional group, so an unconditional statement allowing additive components fails. This does not decide a no-additive-components variant.','C','FGA236 tame étaleness and algebraic-group structure settle the tame case; additive-group calculation refutes the broad reading. The normal/no-additive version and the margin’s exact target remain distinct. No blanket wild openness theorem is attributed to the sources.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'high')
c(253,[255],57,[310],'849–855','multiplication par n de type fini?','Multiplication by a nonzero integer n on the represented Picard functor of a proper family is of finite type.','open on the pages','n=0 must be excluded: its fibre over the identity can be the full infinite-component Picard scheme.','A','Exact match SGA VI XIII Theorem3.6, p.635, for proper X over noetherian S, with n nonzero. Earlier announcement FGA236 complement(iv), p.304; Kleiman2005 Remark6.28 gives the same generalization and points to Kleiman1971 Theorem3.6. Published1962; no claim that the later proof was already written on these sheets.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(254,[255],57,[310],'857–859','propre; projectif','For smooth projective families with geometrically integral fibres, Pic^τ is projective; the text asks whether normality suffices for its projectivity.','open on the pages','Properness and finite type alone do not imply projectivity over an arbitrary base.','C','FGA236 Corollary4.2 and Kleiman2005 Exercise6.18/Answer6.18 give the smooth projective case. The normal-proper variant requires its own ample line bundle or representability theorem and is not inferred from properness.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(255,[255],57,[310],'873–876','fini de S-points d’ordre n: Non','The assertion that Pic^τ has only finitely many S-sections of fixed torsion order is rejected.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P26: for a positive-dimensional abelian variety over infinite k of characteristic p, all first-order identity sections over S=Spec k[ε]/ε² are killed by p because d[p]=0. They form the positive-dimensional k-vector space Lie(A), giving infinitely many p-torsion sections of the finite group scheme A[p].','A','The negative answer is compatible with the nonreduced torsion phenomena in FGA236 §2.9 and Oda1969. The independently supplied example checks the distinction between finite group scheme and finite set of sections. It is not claimed to be the manuscript’s unspecified example.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Oda1969Dieudonne'],['PL-S04'])
c(256,[255],57,[310],'878–879','rang NS majoré: oui','In a proper family over a noetherian base, geometric NS groups are finitely generated with uniformly bounded rank and bounded order of torsion.','stated without proof','Boundedness is not constructibility or local constancy.','A','SGA VI XIII Theorem5.1, p.650, proves finite generation and uniform rank/torsion-order bounds for geometric NS in every proper family over noetherian S. FGA236 complement(vi), p.305, announces both bounds. Kleiman2005 Remark6.19 explicitly distinguishes bounded rank from its generally nonconstructible variation. Published1962.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03','PL-S05'])
c(257,[255,246],57,[310],'887–892','Compatibilité aux spécialisations ???','The final line asks about multiplicity and specialization compatibility of Pic^τ, without specifying a comparison morphism or invariant.','open on the pages','P10 checks only a concrete p-divisibility mechanism; it does not supply a general multiplicity formula.','F','Maulik–Poonen2012 precisely controls geometric NS specialization and gives p-primary defects. FGA236 controls Picard dimension/tangent variation. Neither identifies the unspecified multiplicity/specialization question. This is a source-formulation limit, not a novel unanswered theorem.',['MaulikPoonen2012NS','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S05'],'medium')
c(258,[255],57,[311],'919–925','Pic⁰ plat ⇒ Pic^τ plat','The page claims flatness along the identity component propagates to Pic^τ in the relative Picard setting.','stated without proof','This is not a purely pointwise translation proof unless the required local sections or torsion translations exist.','C','FGA236 Proposition2.10(i), p.235, propagates flatness/smoothness from the identity to Pic^σ and to points of sections. Its definition on p.223 makes σ the prime-to-residue-characteristic torsion component, not all Pic^τ. Thus it proves the tame/characteristic-zero subcase but does not establish the manuscript’s full wild-torsion implication. No counterexample to the exact latter implication was verified in this run; its literature identification remains bounded.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'])
c(259,[225],57,[311],'926–933','Continuité de la torsion de N.S.','The question asks how geometric NS torsion varies in a family and suggests an Igusa modular test family.','open on the pages','Rank, torsion order, reduced components and nonreduced p-torsion are separated; no numerical family is specified on the page.','C','FGA complement(vi) proves uniform boundedness of torsion order. Maulik–Poonen2012 Proposition3.6 gives tame specialization control and Examples3.10–3.12 document p-primary exceptions; only Example3.12’s full mechanism was checked here. A blanket local-constancy conclusion is unsupported.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','MaulikPoonen2012NS'],['PL-S05'],'medium')
c(260,[243],57,[312],'935–949','extension de l’anneau de base','A bilinear form extends along A→A′ by tensoring, with formula (a′⊗x,b′⊗y)↦a′b′⊗Φ(x,y); the ensuing Proposition3 is cut off before its conclusion. Reused printed algebra.','stated without proof','P27 checks balancing and uniqueness of the extended form. No theorem is reconstructed from the truncated Proposition3.','E','The extension-of-scalars formula is elementary and fully determined. The unfinished field/injectivity proposition is classF within this record: its conclusion is not identifiable, despite searches for the reused Bourbaki stratum.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(261,[226,255],57,[313],'950–957','géométriquement localement intègres','The question seeks separatedness of Pic when fibres are geometrically locally integral, weaker than geometrically normal in the adjacent proved setting.','open on the pages','Normal-family properness cannot be applied after simply deleting normality.','C','FGA236 §2.3 treats normal fibres. Kleiman2005 Theorem4.8 proves existence and separatedness for a flat projective family with geometrically integral fibres (projectivity Zariski locally on the base). That is an exact weaker-than-normal positive case. It does not by itself establish the full proper geometrically-locally-integral formulation on the page; this remaining scope is a bounded identification task, not asserted open in2026.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(262,[226,255],57,[313],'955–968','Pic^τ; _n Pic séparé?','The page separately asks separatedness of Pic^τ and n-torsion Pic under separability/reducedness-type fibre conditions, and records a nonseparated double-fibre example.','open on the pages','The drawing and the term séparables do not identify a complete modern hypothesis; torsion subgroup and full Pic must be kept distinct.','F','FGA236 gives normal-family positive results and nonflat/nonreduced examples; Kleiman2005 gives precise representability examples. The exact weaker statement and double-fibre construction remain unidentified from the supplied page.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03','PL-S04'],'medium')
c(263,[226],57,[313],'970–976','α≤α′; λ≥λ′; 2α+μ≤2α′+μ′','The author asks semicontinuity/monotonicity of the abelian, additive and toric dimensions of the reduced connected Picard fibres. The orientation of the specialization primes matters.','open on the pages','The tame Tate rank is 2α+μ, whereas dimension is α+μ+λ; P11 supplies a control against confusing these with tangent dimension.','C','FGA236 Corollary2.7 and Remark2.8, pp.234–235, prove upper semicontinuity of α+μ+λ and constancy without additive components; they give 2α+μ≤2α′+μ′ under their stated separability or separated-torsion hypotheses, with s a specialization of s′. The separate α≤α′ and λ≥λ′ inequalities and unrestricted dimension constancy are explicitly conjectural there. SGA3 II, X Remark8.7, original pp.120–122, states abelian/unipotent semicontinuity for smooth finite-type group schemes and labels the merely flat finite-presentation extension probable. This supplies a smooth subcase, not settlement of every nonsmooth Picard formulation. No universal open-in-2026 assertion follows from this bounded search.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','DemazureGrothendieck1970SGA3II'],['PL-S03','PL-S04','PL-S18'],'medium')
c(264,[226],57,[313],'978–979','Corr_S(X,Y) existe? Oui par Raynaud','For proper families with geometrically integral fibres, the correspondence-class functor is asserted representable, with an affirmative attribution to Raynaud.','stated without proof','The source says classes of correspondences, not the Hilbert scheme of effective correspondences; these are distinct functors.','C','The Picard/rigidification framework identifies a natural candidate correspondence functor. An exact Raynaud theorem for this full relative correspondence functor was not located in the opened material, so the margin is not promoted to a checked bibliographic attribution.',['Kleiman2005Picard','Grothendieck1962FGA236'],['PL-S03'],'medium')
c(265,[220,250],57,[314],'981–989','ouvert de présentation finie','For proper flat finitely presented X/S, the fibrewise τ-trivial locus is an open quasi-compact Picard subfunctor; for each L, the set of τ-trivial fibres is open and retrocompact.','proof sketched','The proof separates generization stability and constructibility. The numerical criterion is matched exactly to FGA complement(viii)(b′).','A','FGA236 complement(v),(viii),(ix), pp.304–306, provides finite type and numerical criteria; Kleiman2005 Remark6.19 gives the general proper finite-type result in the represented setting. The source’s absent criterion(viii′b′) is recovered as a published match, not identified as a missing manuscript sheet.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(266,[220,250],57,[314],'981–989','ouvert et fermé; projectif géom.intègre','In a projective flat finitely presented family with geometrically integral fibres, Pic^τ is also closed, and the τ-trivial-fibre locus of any line bundle is open and closed.','proof sketched','The specialization argument uses constancy of χ(L^m(n)) and the numerical criterion, with all integer m,n as in the published condition.','A','Exact match FGA236 complement(ix), p.306, using(viii)(e); also Kleiman2005 Theorem6.16. Published1962. The manuscript proof’s two missing labels now have checked published endpoints.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236Complement','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(267,[220,227,250],57,[314],'991–993','éliminer projectif; Pic⁰ propre?','The page asks to remove projectivity from closedness and to prove properness of Pic⁰ for normal proper families.','open on the pages','Normal means a flat geometrically normal morphism in the compared theorem; total-space normality alone does not suffice for that substitution.','C','FGA236 §2.3 establishes properness/closedness of Pic⁰ in the normal-proper represented setting. It answers that precise normal-family subquestion. Removal of projectivity under only geometric integrality is a distinct weaker-hypothesis question and is not declared settled by §2.3.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(268,[227],57,[315],'1005–1011','L_t alg.équiv.à0 se prolonge','Over a trait, with proper flat geometrically normal family, universal O-pushforward and a section, a generically algebraically trivial line bundle is asked to extend with algebraically trivial special fibre.','open on the pages','In the represented setting, proper Pic⁰ supplies a section by the valuative criterion; the family section rigidifies it to an actual line bundle. Merely normal total space is not substituted for normal fibres.','A','FGA236 §2.3 gives proper Pic⁰ under the normal-family assumptions; the valuative/rigidification argument supplies the stated extension. The uncertain broader total-space-normal version is not certified. Published1962.',['Grothendieck1962FGA236','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(269,[227],57,[315],'1005–1011','représentable si propre plat à fibres géom.normales, voire intègres?','The final question seeks scheme representability of Pic for proper flat families with geometrically normal or integral fibres, beyond the projective case.','open on the pages','Scheme and algebraic-space representability are explicitly separated.','B','Kleiman2005 Remark4.18 states Artin’s algebraic-space representability and Grothendieck/Raynaud dense-open scheme representability, with counterexamples to unrestricted scheme representability elsewhere in §4. The modern general solution uses algebraic spaces; the stronger scheme-valued formulation requires extra hypotheses. No unconditional scheme existence is claimed.',['Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S03'])
c(332,[212],57,[272],'17–20','si X̂/Ŷ est projectif, X/Y est-il projectif?','For X proper over a noetherian henselian local base in an approximation setting, projectivity after completion is asked to descend to X. The implication follows if completed line bundles can be approximated on the closed fibre.','open on the pages','P32: approximate a completed ample line bundle on the closed fibre; ampleness is open over a proper base, and every open neighborhood of the closed point of a local scheme is the whole base. Properness plus an ample line bundle gives projectivity.','B','Artin1969 supplies finite-order approximation under its explicit hypotheses; Stacks0D2S and0B44 give the exact openness-of-ampleness and proper-plus-ample criteria. P32 verifies their conditional combination. This is not an assertion that projectivity descends along every faithfully flat morphism.',['Artin1969Approximation','Stacks07QX','StacksDivisors'],['PL-S02','PL-S15'])
