# Cote58: formal Lefschetz, local comparison, projective calculations and reused sheets.
c(270,[343],58,[20],'1–37','K′-structure sur V','For a subfield K′⊂K and a K′-subspace V′⊂V, a rational structure means the natural map V′⊗_{K′}K→V is an isomorphism, equivalently the stated universal-extension and basis conditions. Reused printed linear algebra.','proved there','P27 verifies the inverse maps and basis criterion. The excerpt begins mid-proof, so only the visible equivalent conditions are asserted.','E','Standard scalar-extension linear algebra; the reused rédaction358 is not an authorial Picard claim. Searches did not identify the exact edition, but the visible theorem is mathematically complete after restoring its displayed natural map.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(271,[271],58,[22,24,26],'70–113,152–193,238–288','Ass_B(E⊗_A F)','If F is an A-flat B-module, associated primes of E⊗_A F include the union of Ass_B(F/pF) for p∈Ass_A(E); for noetherian A equality holds. The source’s finite/noetherian conditions must be retained.','proved there','The exact associated-prime theorem and its proof were compared with Stacks tag0312; no experimental prime enumeration is substituted for the proof.','E','Exact modern match Stacks Lemma10.65.3(tag0312), including flatness and the noetherian equality clause. The printed source runs26→24→22; it is an ancillary commutative-algebra stratum. No first-edition identification is claimed.',['Stacks0312'],['PL-S10'])
c(272,[272],58,[21],'38–69','Lemme4; connexe en dimension≥h','A connected noetherian X with components of dimension≥h is claimed connected in dimension≥h if its punctured local spectra satisfy the written connectedness condition; a margin strengthens the condition at points whose closures have dimension<h.','proof sketched','The closure-of-a-disconnection argument is read but ends before the contradiction; the uncertain k/h bound prevents claiming the literal proof complete.','A','SGA2 XIII connectedness methods and the exact local/global formalism provide the developed setting. The source itself cites EGA IV geometric connectedness but omits its section number. The marginal pointwise formulation and local dimension convention must be fixed before asserting an exact theorem identity.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S11'],'medium')
c(273,[273,274],58,[23],'114–151','X₁ connexe','The specialization theorem as literally written assumes connectedness, not geometric connectedness, of the generic fibre; under its other stated hypotheses it claims connectedness of the special fibre in the designated dimension.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:split_special_fibre; P1. A=Z_(5), B=A[i], X=P¹_B, h=0: generic fibre is P¹_{Q(i)} connected of dimension1; A is regular/geometrically unibranch, X is proper and integral/dominant; special fibre is two disjoint P¹_F5.','F','The literal ordinary-fibre statement is false, also for every h≥0 by replacing P¹ with P^{h+1} in P1. The same ordinary-fibre wording in SGA2 XIII Corollary2.2 needs a geometric-fibre convention or an additional qualification; its proximity is not a proof of the literal assertion. The complete-local cutting theorem XIII2.1 is a distinct valid statement. No transcription word is changed, and the exact repaired specialization theorem is not assigned a certified first-publication match.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S11'])
c(274,[275],58,[25],'194–237','formellement équidimensionnel; facile(?)','Lemma5’s reduction needs formal equidimensionality of specified local rings in a dominant family; the author labels that input doubtful.','open on the pages','No equidimensionality is inferred merely from integrality. Completion may introduce components unless additional hypotheses control it.','C','SGA2’s local theorems impose complete/regular-ambient hypotheses, and Raynaud1974 I.0 similarly works in a controlled regular-ambient setting. These established results bypass the doubtful arbitrary-noetherian input; they do not prove it with missing hypotheses. No unresolved modern theorem is claimed.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S11'],'medium')
c(275,[276],58,[27],'289–306','K=n+h','The displayed cutting-by-n-equations argument claims loss of at most n in connectedness dimension. Its numerical reduction takes K=n+h.','proof sketched','scripts/calculations.py:cutting_dimension verifies K−n=h and the strict/non-strict endpoint bookkeeping. It does not validate the preceding specialization lemma.','A','The complete-local connectedness theorems developed in SGA2 XIII are the precise later framework; the source’s deduction remains conditional on its previous lemmas. Root’s earlier58:3–17 contains the fuller local theorem and will be linked semantically rather than counted as independent evidence for the false specialization claim.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S11'],'medium')
c(276,[277],58,[27],'299–307','hyperplans; connexité géométrique','The page proposes geometric connectedness statements for ample hyperplane sections in the stated projective setting, with a dimension loss for the number of equations.','open on the pages','Positivity and geometric connectedness hypotheses are part of the reconstruction; they are not optional decorations.','A','SGA2 XII’s projective applications and XIII’s connectedness theorem establish precise ample-section versions. They do not imply the arbitrary merely connected generic-fibre specialization statement at23. The unclear endpoint on this face stays in the reading queue.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S11'])
c(277,[278],58,[27],'307–316','morphisme dans un projectif','The author asks to extend the hyperplane assertion to inverse images under a general morphism to projective space.','open on the pages','Without a fibre-dimension/ampleness condition, a constant map can make an inverse image empty or all of X; a bare morphism is insufficient.','C','SGA2 supplies precise projective/ample Lefschetz statements; Raynaud1974 broadens comparison under explicit depth and properness hypotheses. The unqualified general-morphism extension is not a complete theorem and cannot be certified from a title-level match.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S11'],'medium')
c(278,[278],58,[27],'316–323','analogue local','A local analogue of the hyperplane connectedness argument is proposed, with completion-sensitive hypotheses not fully repeated on this face.','open on the pages','The local dimension shift is the same principal-ideal/cutting calculation, but completion is a separate hypothesis.','A','SGA2 XIII gives the local connectedness framework; its exact formulation, rather than the loose marginal shorthand, is the present established result. No statement about full local π₁ finite generation follows just from connectedness.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Suzuki2023FundamentalGroups'],['PL-S11'])
c(279,[279,280,281],58,[30,31,32],'334–477','généraliser; profondeur','The introduction advertises nonproper formal comparison and existence in a depth-minus-two range, followed by a nine-part programme. Its heavily revised formulas do not specify one complete universal theorem.','open on the pages','Comparison, coherent direct-image finiteness and algebraization are separated. No calculation repairs the missing hypotheses.','A','SGA2 IX–XII develops the advertised programme with exact depth/coherence hypotheses; Michèle Raynaud1974 I.1.1–1.5 gives a stronger unified comparison theorem. Grauert1958/Serre references remain bibliographic blanks; no exact analytic paper is identified merely from the date.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S09','PL-S12'])
c(280,[282,283],58,[33,34],'478–571','LG faible ⇒ LG₀','Weak Lefschetz H⁰ comparison for vector bundles implies injectivity on H¹ for vector bundles by splitting extensions. The generalized claim through arbitrary degree n needs more than injective resolutions outside the vector-bundle class.','proof sketched','P20 proves n=0 using 0→E→V→O→0 and lifting a formal splitting. It records the exact-category gap for arbitrary n.','A','SGA2 X2.3 develops full faithfulness of vector bundles and their finite algebra structures. The n=0 implication is independently proved; no unqualified arbitrary-n endpoint theorem is inferred from it. The surviving source statement is split into valid base case and conditional extension.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(281,[284],58,[34,35],'572–641','0→F→P→F′→0','If every vector bundle embeds into a finite direct sum of chosen generators with locally free cokernel, comparison in the required degrees for those generators propagates to all vector bundles by dimension shifting.','proof sketched','P20 gives the exact-sequence induction and identifies the cokernel/local-freeness requirement. Quasi-projective schemes supply the intended twists via an ample line bundle.','A','SGA2 XII applies twist/depth criteria to obtain Lefschetz comparison. The abstract exact-category argument is independently checked; it is not extended to schemes lacking an ample family or the displayed embeddings.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(282,[284],58,[35],'600–641','deux degrés consécutifs','The five-lemma step transfers isomorphism/injectivity ranges across 0→F→P→F′→0, with the two consecutive cohomological degrees explicitly required.','proved there','P20 writes the long exact diagram and tracks the injective endpoint separately from the isomorphism range.','E','Elementary homological algebra; the SGA2 comparison proofs use the same mechanism. No novel higher-degree conclusion is obtained from a one-degree hypothesis.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(283,[285],58,[36,37],'642–709','probablement pas; LG faible=LG₀=LG','Unrestricted neighborhood stability is cancelled; a qualified equivalence survives under the generating-family/quasi-projectivity assumptions.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P20 distinguishes H⁰ comparison on X, comparison after shrinking, and the germ category. Dropping the neighborhood quantifier is not a proof.','A','SGA2 X’s Lef definition and §2 consequences preserve neighborhood quantifiers. The cancelled general assertion is not revived; the published quasi-projective/depth criteria establish the qualified setting.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(284,[286],58,[33,37],'511–547,674–709','Y∩adhérence{x}=∅ ⇒ x∉Ass O_X','Under the indicated quasi-projective ample/generating-family hypotheses, weak Lefschetz comparison excludes associated points x whose closure is disjoint from Y.','proof sketched','P28: a coherent submodule of O_X supported on the closure of such an associated point, twisted by a sufficiently high ample power, supplies a nonzero section that maps to zero formally; this yields precisely the closure-sensitive necessary condition. The needed extension/generator step is not omitted.','A','SGA2 X and Raynaud1974 I.1.4 express the corresponding local-depth restrictions. The source proof establishes associated-point exclusion, not automatically the stronger depth≥2 converse asked nearby.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S09','PL-S12'],'medium')
c(285,[286,287],58,[33,37],'511–547,674–709','prof≥2?','The author asks whether the Lefschetz condition forces depth≥2 away from Y.','open on the pages','The associated-point argument only supplies depth≥1. P5 proves a useful Hom-depth bound but does not turn this necessary condition into the requested converse.','C','Raynaud1974 I.1.4 gives a sufficient, precisely stratified depth category for full faithfulness; SGA2 XI3.4 gives depth≥2 iff full faithfulness for removal of a closed set, a different restriction functor. The formal-completion converse as written is not identified and is not declared open in general.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz','Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S12'],'medium')
c(286,[289],58,[38],'710–765','algèbres localement libres','Full faithfulness of vector bundles, stable under tensor products, transports finite locally free algebra structures by lifting multiplication and unit and checking identities on tensor powers.','proof sketched','P20 checks multiplication:E⊗E→E, unit:O→E, associativity on E⊗³ and commutativity. No purely categorical equivalence is asserted without the tensor structure.','A','Exact developed consequence SGA2 X2.3, including finite locally free algebra objects. Seminar1962; publication1968.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(287,[289,290],58,[38,39],'710–790','revêtements étales','Effective Lefschetz algebraizes a finite étale formal cover on a neighborhood of Y; étaleness is ensured after removing the closed nonétale locus disjoint from Y.','proof sketched','P20 checks descent of algebra structure and shrinking. Extending the cover to all of X is a separate purity problem.','A','SGA2 X2.3–2.5 proves the neighborhood statement. Neither it nor the source replaces the neighborhood by X without an additional extension/purity hypothesis.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(288,[289,290],58,[38],'710–765','π₀','Under Lefschetz H⁰ comparison, idempotents correspond, giving the stated connected-component comparison in the appropriate germ/formal setting.','proof sketched','P20 uses e²=e in Γ(O); full faithfulness transports that equation. Connectedness/basepoint hypotheses are needed for the subsequent π₁ statement.','A','SGA2 X2.4–2.5 gives the connectedness consequences of Lef/Leff. This is an idempotent argument, not an assertion about all higher homotopy groups.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(289,[290],58,[38,39],'710–790','lim_U π₁(U)','Lef gives surjectivity of the appropriate étale π₁ restriction; Leff identifies π₁(Y) with the inverse limit over neighborhoods U.','proof sketched','Finite étale categories and their fibre functors give the group comparison. The inverse limit cannot be discarded.','A','Exact published framework SGA2 X2.6. Suzuki2023 Theorem1.2 is a distinct local finite-generation theorem for the maximal pro-nilpotent quotient, not a replacement for the full group comparison.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Suzuki2023FundamentalGroups'],['PL-S09','PL-S11'])
c(290,[291],58,[40,41],'791–883','Pic algébrique→Pic formel','Full faithfulness and effectivity of line bundles compare Pic of algebraic neighborhoods with Pic of the formal completion; the neighborhood colimit is essential.','proof sketched','P20/P2 distinguish line-bundle groupoids from isomorphism classes. Completion along Y and restriction to Y are separate arrows.','A','SGA2 XI2.1 gives the algebraic-neighborhood/formal Picard comparison. This is not the same as the H¹/H² criterion for formal Pic→PicY.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(291,[291,293],58,[40,41],'791–883','H¹; H²','For a Cartier formal thickening, H¹ of the successive conormal powers controls uniqueness of line-bundle classes, and H² controls obstructions; vanishing in the requisite range gives injection/bijection on Picard groups.','proof sketched','P2 derives 1+I^n/I^{n+1}≅I^n/I^{n+1} and the H¹/H² long exact sequence, distinguishing units/H⁰ automorphisms. The cancellation at40 remains undecided.','A','SGA2 XI2.2 states the group-level comparison. It supports the corrected Picard-group reading and does not prove the stronger category-level assertion on41.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(292,[292],58,[41],'841–883','pleinement fidèle; équivalence','The literal claim that H¹/H² vanishings alone make restriction of invertible-module categories fully faithful/equivalent is false.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:automorphism_faithfulness; P2. X=Spec k[[t]], Y=Spec k: all relevant positive cohomology vanishes, but automorphisms1 and1+t become equal after restriction.','F','The correct established result is the group-level Pic comparison in SGA2 XI2.2. Full faithfulness also requires the H⁰/unit map to be an isomorphism. The source’s categorical wording, not its valid Pic-class consequence, is refuted.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S09'])
c(293,[294,295,296],58,[41,42,43],'841–975','permanence; cohérent plat','For a projective morphism over a noetherian quasi-projective base, a relatively flat coherent sheaf with proper support should inherit the base’s formal cohomological comparison range. The first statement is cancelled and reformulated.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P21 supplies the proper-perfect-pushforward route and tracks the bounded locally free complex and range. This verifies the precise cohomological formulation, not the full effectivity package.','C','SGA2 IX–XII and Raynaud1974 establish precise comparison/existence frameworks. Under the stated proper-perfect hypotheses the cohomological permanence follows by the recorded derived argument. The exact weakened base-of-flatness and all effectivity quantifiers are not identified with a single opened theorem.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(294,[297],58,[42,43],'884–975','existence; support propre','The page asks to algebraize a relatively flat coherent formal sheaf with proper support, after possibly shrinking the algebraic base around Y.','open on the pages','Cohomological comparison alone does not establish effectivity. P21 explicitly stops before claiming the required global presentation/algebraization.','C','SGA2 IX2.2 and XII3.1 prove local/projective formal-existence theorems with t-regularity and depth≥2; Raynaud1974 extends Lefschetz comparison. These are exact established cases, not a proof of every broad permanence formulation on42–43.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(295,[298],58,[43],'937–967','schémas projectifs plats','The corollaries propose comparison and effectivity for projective flat schemes over the base, via relatively ample bundles and coherent algebra data.','proof sketched','P21 explains which coherent-sheaf result would supply the needed finite presentation and relations; it does not infer effectivity solely from cohomology.','C','SGA2 XII3.1–3.2 gives a checked projective formal-sheaf existence theorem, sufficient for well-specified projective constructions. The broader corollary’s omitted polarization and base hypotheses remain a formulation task.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(296,[298],58,[43],'967–975','module fidèlement plat','A margin suggests reducing a broader coherent-module statement by adjoining a faithfully flat coherent module.','open on the pages','A faithfully flat auxiliary module detects equations after descent, but does not automatically give finite presentation or algebraization of the original module.','F','The opened SGA2 formal-existence theorems have explicit depth/regularity hypotheses; no exact result matching this incomplete auxiliary-module suggestion was identified. It remains an editorial proof strategy, not a certified open theorem.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(297,[299],58,[42,43],'884–975','rétrécir X','For finite-presentation data with proper support, the closed nonflat/nonfree locus has closed image disjoint from Y when the formal restriction is flat/free; shrinking the base removes it.','proof sketched','P21 checks closedness of the relevant bad locus, properness of its image, and why proper support is used.','A','This is the standard shrinking step in the formal-existence setting of SGA2 XII3.1–3.2. It justifies a precise passage from formal-neighborhood flatness to flatness near Y, not a global flatness conclusion.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S13'])
c(298,[300],58,[44],'976–991','omettre quasi-projectivité?','The author hopes to remove quasi-projectivity from the permanence/effectivity statements, with explicit doubt for the projective-scheme variant.','open on the pages','The generating-family and finite locally free resolution mechanisms used earlier may fail without suitable replacements.','C','Raynaud1974 I.0–I.1.5 gives comparison beyond a single projective model under regular-ambient/depth assumptions. The opened results do not identify all the effectivity generalizations proposed on44; the remaining uncertainty concerns exact scope, not a claim of present-day novelty.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz','Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(299,[301],58,[44],'976–991','hypothèses essentielles','The source says relative flatness and proper support are essential and requests examples; it cancels a proposal to omit properness.','stated without proof','No counterexample is drawn on this face. P21 identifies the precise failure points in the pushforward argument; it does not fabricate the missing examples.','C','The checked SGA2 existence theorems retain finiteness/support/depth conditions, and Raynaud’s comparison states its own explicit replacements. These confirm why hypotheses matter but do not identify the exact examples the author intended.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S13'],'medium')
c(300,[302],58,[46],'992–1018','exact; noyau C_Φ','Completion of coherent modules on the punctured local spectrum P along D is exact, with kernel the coherent modules supported on closed subsets missing D; it induces an exact faithful/conservative functor from the Serre quotient.','proved there','P29 checks noetherian completion-flatness, Nakayama at D, kernel support and the kernel criterion for an exact quotient functor. Fullness is not included.','A','SGA2 IX formal comparison and the usual coherent completion theorem supply the framework. The source’s elementary exactness/kernel step is valid independently of its stronger full-faithfulness assertion.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S12'])
c(301,[304,341],58,[46],'1044–1066','Hom(F,G)','For finite modules F,G over a noetherian local ring, depth Hom(F,G)≥min(2,depthG). Thus target depth alone can provide a formal Hom comparison bound.','proof sketched','P5 gives the finite-presentation kernel/image proof using the depth lemma; no depth assumption on F enters.','E','Standard depth-of-Hom algebra, independently proved. Raynaud1974 I.1.4 uses the resulting target-depth category; the Stacks depth-localization lemma supplies the separate local-to-global reduction.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz','Stacks0FCC'],['PL-S12'])
c(302,[341,342],58,[46],'992–1066','Hom(F′,G′)≅Hom(F̂′,Ĝ′)','Let A be noetherian local, a quotient of a regular ring, t-adically complete with t∈m; P=SpecA−{m}, D=V(t)∩P. If coherent G has depth≥2 at every point closed in P outside D, then Hom_P(F,G)→Hom_{P̂}(F̂,Ĝ) is an isomorphism for every coherent F.','proof sketched','P6 verifies every closure-dimension hypothesis of Raynaud I.1.1/1.3 for n=2, uses P5 and Stacks0FCC, then identifies completed internal Hom. This is a proof of the exact clean refinement.','B','Michèle Raynaud1974 I.0, I.1.1 and I.1.3 prove the needed comparison under these complete-local regular-ambient hypotheses. Our explicit reduction shows the source’s clean target-only Hom statement is established; it does not settle the neighboring quotient essential-image claim. Publication1974, no earlier circulation date established here.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz','Stacks0FCC'],['PL-S12'])
c(303,[305],58,[48],'1086–1136','K torsion; t régulier sur C','For coherent F over a noetherian scheme, its ascending t-power torsion stabilizes; quotienting by that torsion gives a coherent module C on which t is a nonzerodivisor.','proved there','P29 checks stabilization and t·c=0⇒a representative lies in a higher torsion kernel, hence c=0. The reduction does not fill the missing depth bound.','E','Standard noetherian dévissage. SGA2 IX2.2 and XII3.1 use the resulting t-regular setting for algebraization; the author’s missing inequality is kept distinct from this valid algebra step.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S12'])
c(304,[303,306],58,[46,48,50],'992–1066,1086–1136,1181–1231','cycles associés de dim0 et1','The source refines its category by excluding small-dimensional associated cycles and adding off-D depth bounds; the inserted dimensions and their scope remain uncertain.','open on the pages','P6 verifies one clean Hom consequence; P7 supplies a depth1 counterexample to the unqualified category. These do not decide the overwritten category definition.','F','Raynaud1974 I.1.4 is a precisely defined category with depth2 conditions on closures missing Y and depth1 on specified generizations. It is a useful exact comparison, but the source’s ambiguous associated-cycle clauses cannot be equated to it without ink.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S12'],'medium')
c(305,[302,307],58,[46,50,52],'1018–1045,1181–1231,1275–1286','dominés|domiciliés; a,b,c′; T₁','The proposed essential image consists of formal coherent modules satisfying extension/domination and associated-cycle conditions; later corollaries invoke undefined a,b,c′ and T₁.','open on the pages','No missing condition is inferred from the desired conclusion. The current transcript does not determine a single essential-image theorem.','F','SGA2 IX2.2 and XII3.1 give specific formal-algebraization criteria. They establish nearby cases but do not recover the missing definitions. After searches in SGA2 and Raynaud1974, this remains a source-identification problem.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S12'],'high')
c(306,[309],58,[46],'1018–1045','ρ pleinement fidèle','If read on the entire Serre quotient of coherent sheaves on P by supports missing D, the full-faithfulness claim is false.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:formal_pole_growth; P7. For A=k[[x,y,z]], t=x, F=G=O_{V(y)∩P}, quotient endomorphisms are k[[x,z]]_(x), formal endomorphisms k((z))[[x]]. Σz^(−n²)xⁿ lies only in the latter by the denominator pole-growth bound.','F','The literal unrestricted reading is refuted. Raynaud1974’s depth-controlled category and the proved clean Hom statement C-SA14-302 remain valid; the example has depth1 at a closed off-D point and therefore does not refute them.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz'],['PL-S12'])
c(307,[308],58,[50,52],'1181–1231,1275–1286','prolongement cohérent','The argument seeks a coherent hull by direct image from an open subscheme, controlled by depth and associated components; c′ plausibly asks extension to the whole completed affine scheme.','proof sketched','Coherence of j_*F is an additional finiteness condition; it is not automatic for coherent F on an open U. The guessed c′ is explicitly our reconstruction.','A','SGA2 IX2.1 and XII5.2–5.6 give precise coherence and finite graded-cohomology criteria. The checked statements explain the method but do not identify undefined a,b,c′,T₁ with certainty.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S12','PL-S14'],'medium')
c(308,[321],58,[57,61],'1384–1459,1616–1659','théorème d’existence; prof…2','For a projective morphism over a noetherian base admitting an ample line bundle, a relatively flat formal coherent module along a relative ample divisor algebraizes when the divisor is fibrewise regular on it and the special-fibre module has depth≥2 at the indicated closed points.','stated without proof','Exact theorem comparison, not a computation. The missing inequality in the reused sheet is not repaired in the transcription. The two handwritten grading additions remain distinct.','A','Exact reused-text match SGA2 XII Theorem3.1 and Corollary3.2, including projectivity, relative regularity and depth bound, followed by coherence and generation of sufficiently positive twists. Seminar1962; publication1968. The printed theorem is not dated by the later reused-sheet placement.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(309,[310,312],58,[56,58,59],'1312–1383,1460–1565','dualité; Ext^{r−i}','The projective calculation translates negative-twist cohomology and localization maps into support/completion conditions for Ext^{r−i}(F,ω) via duality, then into depth conditions.','proof sketched','P30 lays out the exact localization segment and index reversal r−i under duality, keeping each of the four questions separate. The ambient projective-space/dualizing hypotheses are retained.','A','SGA2 XII§1 and §5 develops these duality/depth criteria with explicit ranges. It supports the method, not the source’s false conflation of negative vanishing with positive graded finite generation.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(310,[311],58,[56],'1340–1383','α_i surjectif','The localization exact sequence makes surjectivity of H^{i−1}(X,F(−m))→H^{i−1}(U,F(−m)) equivalent to injectivity, not surjectivity, of α_i:H_Z^i→H^i(X,F(−m)).','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:localization_direction; P4 proves kerα_i=image(boundary). The finite exact-vector-space control checks the logical direction.','F','The exact sequence itself decides the mathematical alarm. SGA2 XII§1 uses the correctly oriented maps; no later theorem justifies the transcribed reversed word. Ink must decide whether the word or arrow was misread.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(311,[313],58,[58],'1460–1528','équivalence avec finite generation positive','The claimed equivalence of negative-twist vanishing and finite generation of the positively graded cohomology module fails in degree0.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:skyscraper_twists; P3. X=U=P^r, F=k(p), p outside Y: ⊕_{m≥0}H⁰(F(m))≅k[x₀] is generated in degree0, but H⁰(F(−m))=k for all m.','F','SGA2 XII5.2–5.6 keeps the relevant finiteness/coherence and vanishing ranges separate. The counterexample checks every literal hypothesis and refutes the equivalence at i=0; it does not refute a properly ranged positive-degree theorem.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(312,[314],58,[58],'1480–1516','H^i(formel)=lim H^i','The identification of formal cohomology with inverse limits of cohomology on thickenings requires a Mittag–Leffler/derived-limit control, not just existence of the inverse system.','proof sketched','P30 records the lim¹ exact-sequence obstruction and the role of eventual ML. No inverse-limit interchange is granted formally.','A','SGA2 IX2.1 proves coherence/comparison with a uniform Mittag–Leffler conclusion under adjacent-degree finiteness hypotheses. The reused Roos material on57:296 is contextually relevant but does not independently supply every formal-system hypothesis.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Laudal1965Limits'],['PL-S07','PL-S14'])
c(313,[315,320],58,[58],'1516–1528','condition(*) près de Z','If condition(*) controls depth only at points near Z=X−U, then for U=X it is vacuous and cannot imply the advertised algebraic/formal H⁰ comparison.','false as stated','scripts/calculations.py:skyscraper_twists; P3. F=k(p) supported away from Y has H⁰=k but formal restriction0, with Z empty.','F','SGA2 XII’s correct criteria distinguish boundary finiteness from depth along/off the formal divisor. The source’s scope of(*) must be checked on ink; the vacuous-boundary reading is refuted independently.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(314,[316],58,[59],'1529–1565','conditions suffisantes non nécessaires','The author notes that the displayed depth bounds give sufficient conditions for formal comparison and need not be necessary; the H⁰ argument uses the relevant depth2 threshold.','stated without proof','P6 proves a precise sufficient H⁰/Hom theorem and P3/P7 show why weaker blanket conditions fail. No converse is inferred.','A','Raynaud1974 I.1.1–1.5 provides exact sufficient comparison bounds; SGA2 XII gives the earlier projective criteria. These match the source’s warning against reversing a sufficient implication.',['Raynaud1974Lefschetz','Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S12','PL-S14'])
c(315,[317],58,[59],'1552–1565','F(m) | F(−m)','The final restriction map changes the sign of the twist relative to the negative-twist argument. The mathematical consequences differ.','open on the pages','P3 gives a concrete degree0 test distinguishing positive graded finite generation from negative vanishing. No sign is silently repaired.','F','The correctly ranged positive/negative statements are separated in SGA2 XII§§1,5. The manuscript sign cannot be identified from mathematical convenience and remains a scan-reading problem.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(316,[318,320],58,[60],'1566–1615','cohérence R^i g_*; module gradué','Boundary local-cohomology conditions control coherence of R^i g_* and finite generation of positive graded cohomology, with an additional degree0 requirement for g_*F.','proof sketched','P30 separates R^0 from higher direct images and explains why the source’s endpoint cannot be inferred from higher-degree vanishing.','A','SGA2 XII5.2–5.6 is the checked published development of the coherence/graded finiteness criteria, with the ambient and degree ranges stated there. This is an exact method/range comparison, not approval of every erased inequality.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(317,[319,320],58,[62],'1660–1705','i>0; F=g_*(F|U)','For i>0, positive-twist vanishing relates to vanishing of R^i g_*; the aggregated depth criterion also assumes F=g_*(F|U), eliminating the first two local-cohomology obstructions.','proof sketched','P30 uses 0→H_Z⁰(F)→F→g_*F|U→H_Z¹(F)→0 and higher R^i g_*≅H_Z^{i+1}; the i=0 clause is not dropped.','A','SGA2 XII5.2–5.6 proves the correctly ranged statements. The source’s separate F=g_* hypothesis is mathematically essential to the combined depth reformulation.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S14'])
c(318,[322],58,[47,49],'1067–1085,1137–1180','idéaux inversibles; projectifs rang1','Over a domain A with fraction field K, invertible fractional ideals are finite projective A-modules of rank1 embedded in K; choosing a K-basis of P⊗K realizes every rank1 projective module as such an ideal. Reused print.','proved there','P27 proves both directions, the evaluation inverse and independence up to multiplication by K*.','E','Classical invertible-module theory; Stacks divisor/Picard definitions provide the modern formulation. The printed sheet is not new manuscript research. The exact Bourbaki edition is not identified.',['StacksDivisors'],['PL-S17'])
c(319,[322],58,[51,53],'1232–1274,1287–1304','rang; mineurs','The linear-system criterion uses ranks of coefficient/augmented matrices and vanishing/nonvanishing of the appropriate minors over a field. Reused print.','proved there','scripts/calculations.py:rank_minor_checks; P19 states the general rank-solvability proof, and finite exact examples check the indicated boundary cases.','E','Elementary linear algebra, long established independently of the manuscript. No geometric Picard statement follows from this physical reuse. Source searches identified no exact edition, which does not affect the checked identity.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(320,[322],58,[51,53],'1232–1274,1287–1304','identité de Jacobi','The determinant/cofactor identity is the Jacobi complementary-minor identity, with the required sign and determinant power. Reused print.','proved there','scripts/calculations.py:jacobi_cofactor; exact symbolic polynomial residual0. P19 derives it from adj(A)=det(A)A⁻¹ and extends by polynomial identity to singular A.','E','Classical determinant algebra; the identity is independently checked. The source edition is not identified and the formula is not attributed to Grothendieck as new research.',['StacksAlgebra'],['PL-S17'])
c(321,[323],58,[63],'1706–1741','Brauer et Witt; E[H] décomposée','For characteristic-zero E, if E[H] is a product of matrix algebras over commutative field extensions for every hyperelementary subgroup H of a finite G, then E[G] is likewise decomposed. The source’s induction sum is missing its Σ sign.','proof sketched','P31 checks the character-induction/rational-realizability argument and distinguishes decomposed from split over E itself. No missing R_p definition is imported into this proposition.','E','The sheet explicitly credits Brauer/Witt. Brauer1951 §2.6 p.241 defines exactly the written hyperelementary groups; its Theorem p.250 detects every prime part of a Schur index on such subgroups. This proves the decomposability criterion; Fontaine1971 §8 supplies the refined Witt framework; the exact detached source is probably related to Serre1960 but that article’s body was not accessed and no identification is certified.',['Brauer1951GroupRings','Fontaine1971Decomposition'],['PL-S19'],'medium')
c(322,[323],58,[63],'1742–1753','G vérifie(R_p); Q_ℓ[G] décomposée','The cut-off Theorem3 proof claims decomposability of Q_ℓ[G] for ℓ≠p when G satisfies the undefined condition(R_p).','proof sketched','The fragment omits the definition and remainder of proof. Fontaine’s R_p means a cyclic prime-to-p quotient with normal p-subgroup; that is a compared definition, not an ink restoration.','B','Fontaine1971, Sur la décomposition..., §6 Corollary1 p.168, §7 Theorems5/5′ pp.175–177 and §8.2 Corollary1 p.179 yield the ℓ≠p decomposability result for its explicitly defined R_p groups (normal p-subgroup, cyclic prime-to-p quotient). The absent manuscript definition prevents certifying that hypothesis as an ink reading. The handwritten packet contains reused print; it is not evidence of an original Picard theorem or of its date.',['Fontaine1971Decomposition'],['PL-S19'],'medium')
c(323,[324,325],58,[65,67],'1754–1835,1967–2023','Spec S[z]; cône projectif','Spec S[z] on65 and the projective cone on67 are distinct schemes; the primed/tilde boxed Pic=0 assertions cannot all be identified without their missing definitions.','open on the pages','P17/P18 distinguish affine grading contraction, punctured cone, total line-bundle space and projective cone; their Picard groups differ.','F','EGA II §§8.3–8.8 provides exact cone models; Traverso1970 gives the seminormal affine homotopy theorem. Neither can decide undefined primes/tildes on65. The literal Spec is not rewritten as Proj.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII','Traverso1970Seminormality'],['PL-S15'])
c(324,[325,326],58,[65],'1754–1835','Pic du cône=0','For a positively graded reduced seminormal finite-type k-algebra R with R₀=k, the affine cone SpecR has Pic=0. Seminormality is an explicit modern sufficient hypothesis, absent from the doubtful boxes.','open on the pages','P17 proves the grading contraction using R→R[t], r_d↦r_dt^d, evaluation0/1 and Picard homotopy invariance; no assertion about every singular cone follows.','B','Traverso1970 Theorem3.6 proves Pic(A)≅Pic(A[T]) for reduced noetherian seminormal A with finite normalization (in particular finite type over k). The affine-cone conclusion is our stated deduction. Publication1970, no earlier circulation date established.',['Traverso1970Seminormality'],['PL-S15'])
c(325,[328],58,[66],'1836–1857','H¹(X,O_X)','The surrounding multiplicative-unit sequence yields Pic=H¹(O_X^*); replacing O_X^* by O_X gives a different additive-cohomology group.','false as stated','P14/P2 derive the units sequence and distinguish H¹(O) from Pic. The mathematical alarm does not determine the actual handwritten star.','F','SGA2 XI’s Picard formalism and Ferrand’s units/gluing theory give the correct multiplicative coefficient. The source symbol remains undecided on ink.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2','Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08','PL-S16'])
c(326,[329],58,[66],'1858–1884','factoriel⇒parafactoriel','Categorical parafactoriality of a noetherian local ring requires depth≥2 and Pic of the punctured spectrum0. The unrestricted factorial⇒parafactorial claim fails in dimension1.','false as stated','P16: for a DVR A, PicA=PicFracA=0 but A*→FracA* misses a uniformizer; the restriction category is not full. In dimension≥2 a noetherian factorial local ring has the required depth and punctured Pic vanishing.','A','SGA2 XI3.4–3.5 gives the exact criterion, and XI3.13 proves local complete intersections of dimension≥4 parafactorial. The repaired dimensional assertion is established; the unrestricted categorical reading is refuted.',['Grothendieck1968SGA2'],['PL-S16'])
c(327,[330],58,[66],'1885–1966','g=1+Σg_i+Σn_s−ν','For a reduced connected proper curve over an algebraically closed field, p_a=Σg(normalized components)+Σδ_s−number of components+1. The defect n_s must be a normalization length.','proof sketched','P15 derives Euler characteristics from0→O_C→ν_*O_C̃→Q→0. scripts/calculations.py:nodal_graph_genus checks irreducible node, tree, cycle and multicomponent controls.','E','Classical normalization/genus formula, recovered independently. Ferrand2003 supplies the precise pinching/module framework. Counting only branches would miss cuspidal/additive defects; the nearby xy=0 in three variables is a surface, not a curve.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08','PL-S16'])
c(328,[330,331],58,[66],'1885–1966','courbe nodale; graphe','For a connected nodal curve, the affine part of Pic⁰ is a torus of rank b₁ of the dual graph, and p_a=Σg_i+b₁. This is the nodal specialization of the normalization computation.','proof sketched','P14/P15 plus nodal_graph_genus: node-gluing parameters modulo component rescaling give coker(G_m^V→G_m^E), rank|E|−|V|+1.','E','Established generalized-Jacobian/pinching calculation. The proof is independently supplied using the opened Ferrand descent framework. It does not imply any pro-tower/arithmetic statement from the unavailable external projects.',['Ferrand2003Pinching'],['PL-S08','PL-S16'])
c(329,[332,333],58,[67],'1967–1989','Pic(X′)=Pic(X)⊕Z','For the projective-bundle model X′=P(O_X⊕L) over connected X, Pic(X′)≅Pic(X)⊕Z; the initial equality omitting Z is cancelled.','corrected by himself later (both places)','P18 uses fibre degree to split by O(1); restriction to a section identifies the PicX factor. The direct-sum formula is for the projective bundle, not the affine total space.','E','EGA II projective-bundle/cone formalism underlies the formula; Kleiman2005 Answer4.15 supplies the Picard calculation for projective-space bundles through degree and base change. The source’s own correction is retained.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII','Kleiman2005Picard'],['PL-S15'])
c(330,[332,333],58,[67],'1967–2002','Pic Ĉ=Z','For a connected proper X over k with Γ(O_X)=k and the specified full-section-ring projective cone, Pic(Ĉ)=Z follows from its projective-bundle contraction when f_*O=O_Ĉ and the pulled-back hyperplane bundle is the generator trivial on the exceptional section.','proof sketched','P18 proves injection via projection formula, computes restriction to the exceptional section, and identifies the descended O(1) generator. The hypotheses are part of the statement.','A','EGA II8.7.5(p.176) requires eventual S_{n+1}=S₁S_n and identifies the vertex inverse image;8.8.3–8.8.4 gives the proper/projective contraction;8.8.6(p.181) gives g_*O=O under O_Y=f_*O_X. These exact original pages were read and visually checked. The Pic conclusion is the recorded deduction, not a verbatim EGA theorem. Publication1961.',['Grothendieck1961EGAII'],['PL-S15'])
c(331,[334,327],58,[67],'2003–2023','surjectif(?)','For the total space E of a line bundle over X, pullback PicX→PicE is split injective by the zero section. The page asks surjectivity; reduced seminormal finite-type X is a sufficient modern setting.','open on the pages','P17 proves split injectivity and the seminormal surjection by local affine homotopy invariance and unit descent. A nonseminormal affine cusp alone is not a counterexample to the exact projective setup.','B','Traverso1970 Theorem3.6 gives affine homotopy invariance under seminormality/finite normalization; the line-bundle-total-space case follows by the stated descent proof. The source does not assert seminormality, so unrestricted surjectivity is not certified.',['Traverso1970Seminormality'],['PL-S15'])
